## 3. Important Code & Deep Dive

### 3.1 Preventing Email Enumeration (`auth.service.ts`)
```typescript
const user = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
if (!user) {
  throw ApiError.unauthorized('Invalid email or password');
}

const isPasswordValid = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.passwordHash);
if (!isPasswordValid) {
  throw ApiError.unauthorized('Invalid email or password');
}
```
*Why this matters:* If we returned "Email not found" vs "Wrong password", an attacker could feed a dictionary of emails to determine which accounts exist on the platform. Returning the identical message prevents user enumeration.

### 3.2 Dynamic Test Case Visibility (`problem.service.ts`)
```typescript
export async function getProblemById(id: string, isAdmin = false) {
  const problem = await prisma.problem.findUnique({
    where: { id },
    include: {
      testCases: {
        where: isAdmin ? {} : { isSample: true }, // Users only see samples
        orderBy: { orderIndex: 'asc' },
        select: {
          id: true,
          input: true,
          expectedOutput: true,
          isSample: true,
          orderIndex: true,
          ...(isAdmin ? { timeLimitMs: true, memoryLimitMb: true } : {}),
        },
      },
    },
  });
  ...
}
```
*Why this matters:* Even if a clever user examines network responses in Chrome DevTools, hidden test cases are filtered out in PostgreSQL by Prisma. They never leave the server.

### 3.3 Multi-Target Zod Middleware (`validate.ts`)
```typescript
export function validate(schema: ZodSchema, source: 'body' | 'query' | 'params' = 'body') {
  return (req: Request, _res: Response, next: NextFunction): void => {
    try {
      const parsed = schema.parse(req[source]);
      req[source] = parsed;
      next();
    } catch (error) {
      if (error instanceof ZodError) {
        const details = error.errors.map((e) => ({
          field: e.path.join('.'),
          message: e.message,
        }));
        throw ApiError.badRequest('Validation failed', details);
      }
      throw error;
    }
  };
}
```
*Why this matters:* Zod coerces and validates string query parameters (e.g., `?page=2` becomes `number 2`) and guarantees type safety before entering controllers.

